  "generated_text": "1. The formula for the volume of a sphere is \\( V = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi r^3 \\).\n2. For the first snowball with radius 2 inches:\n   \\[\n   V_1 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (2)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (8) = 8\\pi\n   \\]\n3. For the second snowball with radius 3 inches:\n   \\[\n   V_2 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (3)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (27) = 36\\pi\n   \\]\n4. For the third snowball with radius 5 inches:\n   \\[\n   V_3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (5)^3 = \\frac{4}{3} \\pi (125) = 200\\pi\n   \\]\n5. Now, add the volumes of all three snowballs:\n\\[\n8\\pi + 36\\pi + 200\\pi = 244\\pi\n\\]\n\n\\(\\boxed{244\\pi}\\)",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[244*pi, '244\\\\pi']",
    "gold_answer": "A sphere with radius $r$ has volume $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi r^3$.  Thus, the snowballs with radius 2, 3, and 5 inches have volumes $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(2^3)$, $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(3^3)$, and $\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(5^3)$ cubic inches respectively.  The total volume of snow used is thus  \\begin{align*}\n\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(2^3)+\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(3^3)+\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(5^3)&=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(2^3+3^3+5^3)\\\\\n&=\\frac{4}{3}\\pi(8+27+125)\\\\\n&=\\boxed{\\frac{640}{3}\\pi}.\\end{align*}",
    "is_correct": false
  },
